---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { SITE } from '../site.config';
---

<Base title="About" description={`About ${SITE.name}: research across software security, quantitative finance and machine learning.`}>
  <article class="prose">
    <header class="page-head">
      <p class="eyebrow">About</p>
      <h1>{SITE.name}</h1>
      <p class="lede">I'm a researcher working across software security, quantitative finance and machine learning. Online I go by <code>{SITE.handle}</code>.</p>
    </header>

    <h2>Security</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Reverse engineering and software protection:</strong> authorized analysis of .NET internals, obfuscators and code virtualizers, anti-tamper and licensing mechanisms, plus good-faith vulnerability research.</li>
      <li><strong>Application and infrastructure security:</strong> authorized penetration testing, web application security and Windows internals.</li>
      <li><strong>Malware analysis and incident response.</strong></li>
    </ul>
    <p>Security vulnerabilities I find in third-party products are reported privately to the vendor under my <a href="/disclosure-policy/">disclosure policy</a>, with client approval where encountered during an engagement. Published results are on the <a href="/advisories/">advisories</a> page.</p>

    <h2>Quantitative finance</h2>
    <p>Quantitative research on systematic trading, treated as a mathematical problem: statistical modelling of market data, strategy design, and validation on data the model has never seen. The work draws on probability, statistics and optimization, and it gets the same scrutiny as a security finding: a result counts only if it can be reproduced.</p>

    <h2>Machine learning and AI</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Machine learning research:</strong> methods and experiments. I write about methods and results; the models themselves are not published.</li>
      <li><strong>AI-assisted security research:</strong> agent-driven analysis pipelines with verifiable, provenance-checked output.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>How I work</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Client engagements and tests of live or remote systems run only under written authorization, a defined scope and Rules of Engagement from the owner.</li>
      <li>Independent security research is conducted in good faith on lawfully obtained software in isolated local test environments, without accessing third-party infrastructure or user data.</li>
      <li>Client findings stay with the client. Security vulnerabilities in third-party products are reported to the vendor through <a href="/disclosure-policy/">coordinated disclosure</a>, and only with written client approval if encountered during a client engagement.</li>
      <li>Public write-ups and advisories omit client-identifying details, weaponizable exploits, circumvention tools and proprietary vendor material.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Work with me</h2>
    <p>I take on authorized protection assessments and security audits. The <a href="/services/">services page</a> explains what I offer and how an engagement runs. For everything else, use the <a href="/contact/">contact page</a>.</p>
  </article>
</Base>
